#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/id #uuid "af260af2-fd05-54bc-8f6f-a5612cb922db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7048" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nthe formula for the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157007 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/id #uuid "f1eaa2b8-544c-5217-82a5-8ec0a311c5a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7049" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b$?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda=-4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v} te^{\\lambda t} + \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\,)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w}$, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(A - λI)w &= v \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 + 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 + 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\\\ w_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\\\ w_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n0w_{1} + 1w_{2} = 1; 0w_{1} + 0w_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n$w_2 = 1$.\n\nThe variable $w_1$ is a free variable. Setting $w_1 = 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0, b=1$, and\n\n$$\na+b=1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157008 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/id #uuid "38f7a261-b58c-5ce1-ab77-6bfb5ce40b19" :question/math-academy-id "e-7050" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda=3$.*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda=3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1 \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}t e^{\\lambda t} + \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t})\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w}$, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(A - λI)w &= v \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 - 3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7 - 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\\\ w_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\\\ w_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n-4w_{1} - 8w_{2} =-2; 2w_{1} + 4w_{2} = 1\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_1 =\\dfrac{1}{2} - 2 w_2\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_2$ is a free variable. Setting $w_2 = 0$, we get\n\n$$\nw_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2c_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}c_{2} \\\\ c_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2c_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}c_{2} \\\\ c_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} - 2(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}te^{3t} + \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}te^{3t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(4t - 3)e^{3t} \\\\ (1 - 2t)e^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e349736-e032-5c48-ac0e-33d84005d257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155226} {:answer/id #uuid "df12b381-f17f-5822-99ee-98fc05adaaf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155227} {:answer/id #uuid "f26dd776-3f42-58e2-990d-79c5942c99d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155228} {:answer/id #uuid "158d0b81-5987-5088-9a41-4b14ff7956a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155229} {:answer/id #uuid "49442852-dfad-553f-83ef-e8b645fd8491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df12b381-f17f-5822-99ee-98fc05adaaf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155227} :answer-field/id #uuid "cba6f085-8040-5c8d-a9c0-beeff1f1f4ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155225}] :question/id #uuid "f38771d6-22f6-588d-95e5-d42dd40123e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-325120" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$.\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t} + c_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}te^{2t} + \\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t}), c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$,\n\nwhat is the value of $a + b$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab481a2e-415d-58d2-a997-4b68fd69003a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2(1 - t)e^{4t} \\\\ - (1 + 2t)e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155238} {:answer/id #uuid "d3724f08-8a3a-5f41-b9db-f993a966d4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2(1 - t)e^{4t} \\\\ -e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155239} {:answer/id #uuid "fd53afcd-195a-52a2-b1a2-4890ba8b57bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(2 + t)e^{4t} \\\\ - (1 + 2t)e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155240} {:answer/id #uuid "1f1953aa-7928-552c-ae5c-0ac1b676a1f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2e^{4t} \\\\ -e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155241} {:answer/id #uuid "701eb902-2e4a-562b-8d35-40c92adab2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2e^{4t} \\\\ - (1 + 2t)e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3724f08-8a3a-5f41-b9db-f993a966d4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2(1 - t)e^{4t} \\\\ -e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155239} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ad6a1bb-d19b-5480-a9e2-344944bf4ade" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb